    <Folder>
      <name>Textbook Specific Film Sites</name>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tama River Area (Adrift in Tokyo)</name>
        <description>Adrift in Tokyo (Satoshi Miki) Popular location for walking scenes in the film.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.6311827,35.6191748,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tokyo Station (Aggtesuko)</name>
        <description>Aggretsuko (Rareko) Shibuya – Many city shots are inspired by the Shibuya area.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7658469,35.6818718,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Roppongi Hills Mori Tower (Tokyo Drift)</name>
        <description>Fast and Furious: Tokyo Drift (Justin Lin)</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7292066,35.6607345,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Kabukicho, Shinjuku</name>
        <description>First Love (Takashi Miike) A significant portion of the film takes place in this entertainment district.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7007103,35.6947405,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tokyo Tower (Godzilla and others)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Godzilla Minus One (2023, Takashi Yamazaki)<br>And Godzilla Statue!]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.745484,35.6585696,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Godzilla Square!</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7599204,35.6730915,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Shinjuku Omoide Yokocho (Midnight Diner)</name>
        <description>The alleyways in Midnight Diner are very similar to the ones found in Omoide Yokocho, a small, narrow street filled with small restaurants serving classic Japanese dishes. This is a common setting for intimate late-night meals, reflecting the tone of the series.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.6975192,35.6917377,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Jindaiji Temple (Adrift in Tokyo)</name>
        <description>This ancient temple, one of Tokyo’s oldest, is featured in Adrift in Tokyo.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.5503942,35.6675048,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Rikkyo University (Tokyo Ghoul)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Rikkyo University (Kamii University)<br>The real-life inspiration for Kamii University, where Kaneki and Hide attend.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.702509,35.7305152,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Shinjuku Station East Exit Smoking Area (Tokyo Ghoul)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Shinjuku Station East Exit<br>This busy hub features in a key scene when Kaneki goes to meet Touka.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7007164,35.6922643,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Kabukichō (Tokyo Ghoul)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Kabukicho Crossing<br>Known as the nightlife district of Shinjuku, Kabukicho features in multiple scenes of Tokyo Ghoul.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7019065,35.6936049,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Yotsuya Station (Your Name)</name>
        <description>Featured multiple times in the film, this station is near many key locations.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7302183,35.6861525,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Takeshita Street Entrance Arch (Harajuku Station) (JJK)</name>
        <description>Another key spot in Jujutsu Kaisen, where the trio meets Nobara.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7032327,35.6715743,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Harajuku Station (JJK)</name>
        <description>A prominent location in Jujutsu Kaisen, where Itadori, Fushiguro, and Gojo meet.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7029143,35.671599,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Edo-Tokyo Open Air Architectural Museum (Spirited Away) (Samurai) (Edo)</name>
        <description>The museum inspired the bathhouse in Spirited Away.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.5125434,35.7155722,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Adachi City (Shoplifters)</name>
        <description>The film captures the quieter, more suburban neighborhoods of Tokyo.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.8044129,35.775017,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tokyo Station (First Ward)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[The 1st Ward is where the CCG Main Office is located, and it serves as the headquarters for anti-Ghoul operations. It’s based on Chiyoda, home to major governmental buildings and Tokyo Station.<br>Notable Spot: Tokyo Station, a historic station with modern vibes, representing the CCG's power and influence.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7671248,35.6812362,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Roppongi (3rd Ward)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Description: The 3rd Ward features Roppongi, a famous nightlife district and key location in Tokyo Ghoul where Kaneki and Rize go on their fateful date.<br>Notable Spot: Roppongi Hills, known for its luxury shopping and skyline views, including a perfect view of Tokyo Tower at night.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7345469,35.6618586,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tokyo Skytree (6th Ward)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Description: In Tokyo Ghoul, the 6th Ward is known for its high-tech vibe, which is mirrored by Taitō’s Tokyo Skytree, the tallest tower in Japan, representing a modern and futuristic Tokyo.<br>Notable Spot: Tokyo Skytree — a great spot for viewing Tokyo’s cityscape and tying in the "Neo-Tokyo" feel.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.8107004,35.7100627,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tokyo Metropolitan Government Building (JJK)</name>
        <description>Description: This towering building, located in Shinjuku, appears in Jujutsu Kaisen and is an iconic structure in Tokyo. It’s featured when major city-wide incidents unfold in the series.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.6916863,35.6894807,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Aoyama Cemetery (JJK)</name>
        <description>Description: This location serves as the setting for darker moments in Jujutsu Kaisen. Aoyama Cemetery, known for its peaceful setting amidst the city, provides the backdrop for eerie scenes in the show.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7220659,35.6656277,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Senkaku Junction (JJK)</name>
        <description>Description: The multi-level highway junction is used for intense fight sequences in Jujutsu Kaisen. Its twisting roads provide a unique urban battle setting.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.6963802,35.6916719,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Yoyogi National Gymnasium (JJK)</name>
        <description>Description: This landmark appears during the events of the series, representing a key battle arena.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7001458,35.6676472,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Hotel Gajoen Tokyo (Sprited Away)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Hotel Gajoen in Tokyo is believed to have inspired the design of the bathhouse in Spirited Away. Known as one of Tokyo's "Museum Hotels," the hotel features stunning, traditional Japanese architecture with lavish interior designs, ornate rooms, and unique artistic displays, closely resembling the detailed design of the bathhouse in the film.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7137936,35.6317881,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>NTV Big Clock designed by Hayao Miyazaki</name>
        <description>Description: A giant mechanical clock designed by Hayao Miyazaki himself, this Ghibli-themed clock is located outside Nippon Television Tower in Shiodome. Every hour, the clock comes alive with a whimsical performance.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7599544,35.6641255,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Suga Shrine (Your Name)</name>
        <description>The staircase where the characters meet in Your Name is located near Suga Shrine in Yotsuya, Tokyo. This spot has become a pilgrimage site for fans of the movie.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7224783,35.6852851,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Asukayama Park (Spirited Away)</name>
        <description>Old anime style yellow bus that can be used for scene recreations.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7388228,35.7504718,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tamagawa Aqueduct Greenway (Garden of Words)</name>
        <description>Scene: The shaded pathways of the Tamagawa Aqueduct serve as the setting for reflective walks in The Garden of Words.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.6631049,35.67116,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Nakano Broadway (Steins;gate)</name>
        <description>Scene Description: Nakano Broadway is a hidden gem often featured for its retro shops and otaku culture. In Steins;Gate, it reflects the world of collectors and enthusiasts.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1727-673AB7-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.6656524,35.7092475,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
