    <Folder>
      <name>Food</name>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Benitsuru</name>
        <description>FLUFFY Pancakes—a pancake shop that perfectly balances salt and sweetness in its pancakes, making it a representative sweets store for those sightseeing in Asakusa.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7918882,35.7119914,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Kanda Yabu Soba</name>
        <description>a favorite of mine; a soba restaurant renowned for its traditional Edo-style noodles. The restaurant, rebuilt following a fire, continues to serve various soba dishes, including the Kamo-san ban soba featuring thick slices of duck and the ten-seiro soba paired with crispy shrimp.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1640-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7686863,35.6970004,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name><![CDATA[KEY'S CAFÉ 秋葉原SEEKBASE店]]></name>
        <description>A coffee shop within walking distance in Akihabara with wifi and good group seating. Open early and is a good place to study. </description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1534-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7734777,35.7013398,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Himuka Shokudo</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Shokudo (食堂, shokudō) are casual restaurants or cafeterias that serve a variety of inexpensive Japanese dishes. Many of them are mom-and-pop, hole-in-the-wall type restaurants where the locals eat. They are numerous across Japan in urban centers and on the countryside. <br><br>A meal at a shokudo usually cost around 800 to 1500 yen. ]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7713926,35.7023996,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Suzukien Asakusa</name>
        <description><![CDATA[the world's richest matcha gelato with seven intensity levels, catering to matcha newcomers and aficionados. Established through a collaboration between Suzukien, a historic tea shop from 1853, and Nanaya, a matcha sweets factory, this venue is a must-visit for its unique range of matcha gelato flavors.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7969391,35.7163436,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tesshin</name>
        <description>teppanyaki, particularly featuring premium Kuroge Wagyu beef. Their signature dish is the Kuroge Wagyu Steak, priced at 1,380 JPY plus tax, seasoned with salt, kombu, and wasabi, offering a quintessential Japanese flavor profile.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1553-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7067632,35.6913441,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Yakiniku King</name>
        <description><![CDATA[A top-rated all-you-can-eat Yakiniku restaurant in Tokyo, Yakiniku King specializes in Japanese BBQ and offers a wide selection of meats for grilling. It's known for its quality, variety, and value, making it a must-visit for meat lovers. In 2022, it beat other well-known chains like Gyu-Kaku to rank as the number one Yakiniku spot. The restaurant also caters to tourists with English touchscreen menus.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1553-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7929484,35.7123703,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Fruits Parlor Gotō</name>
        <description><![CDATA[—café with sweet fruit parfaits near the old Hanayashiki amusement park. The menu, which changes seasonally, features delights such as grape and melon parfaits during summer, alongside other sweets like pancakes, kakigori shaved ice, and ice cream. It's a perfect spot for those seeking seasonal fruit desserts.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1607-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7938062,35.7157529,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Buta-Daigaku Shimbashi</name>
        <description>—Known for its pork bowls, which cost approximately 600 Yen for a small size, this is a must-visit for savory pork bowl lovers. It is located near Shibuya.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7569785,35.6663124,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Ginza Kagari - Soba</name>
        <description><![CDATA[— (Prepare to wait) famous for its sophisticated take on chicken ramen. The restaurant's signature dish, tori-paitan, features a creamy, rich broth derived from chicken, which provides a velvety texture and deep flavor.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1640-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7613736,35.6712211,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Hobo Shinjuku Norengai</name>
        <description><![CDATA[located a short 6-minute walk from Odakyu Shinjuku Station or Minami-Shinjuku Station, offers a collection of restaurants housed in traditional Japanese houses. This area provides a more classic and atmospheric dining experience compared to the modern establishments around Shinjuku Station. The term "Noren-gai" translates to "shop curtain town close to Shinjuku," referring to the traditional curtain (noren) that signifies an open shop. These 8 renovated old folk houses host a variety of dining options, not limited to Japanese cuisine.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7033933,35.6830213,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Soba House Konjiki-Hototogisu</name>
        <description>—(Prepare to wait) a ramen restaurant in Shinjuku, Tokyo, celebrated for its unique and sophisticated flavors. Awarded a Michelin star in 2018, this ramen establishment is renowned for its shoyu soba.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1640-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7082917,35.6887843,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Ramen Sugimoto</name>
        <description>—(Prepare to wait) ramen shop located in Nakano, Tokyo. This shop offers a unique ramen experience that both locals and visitors highly praise. The restaurant is known for its special shio (salt) ramen, which includes a generous topping of chashu pork, a perfectly cooked egg, and homemade wontons.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1640-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.5153279,35.5502952,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Shiro-Hige’s Cream Puff Factory</name>
        <description>Nestled in Setagaya, this unique spot Offers Totoro-shaped cream puffs starting at 400 Yen. It is also a spot for Studio Ghibli fans and sweet tooth indulgers.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1607-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.6627485,35.6589542,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Hakushū Teppanyaki</name>
        <description>This establishment shines with its premium Japanese Wagyu Beef prepared teppanyaki-style in Shibuya. It is celebrated for top-notch Kobe beef.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1553-FFD600-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7009933,35.6562855,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name><![CDATA[Kicks Cafe&Bar]]></name>
        <description>A great local place to get a burger.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7689344,35.7060035,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>YAKITORI Nonotori Nihonbashi</name>
        <description>Yakitori Nonotori in Nihonbashi offers an exceptional dining experience, particularly through its Premium Omakase Course, which features seasonal, expertly grilled skewers and side dishes. The restaurant is praised for its use of binchotan charcoal, which imparts a unique flavor to the yakitori, and its signature tamago kake gohan (rice with eggs cooked in an earthenware pot). Many diners appreciate the combination of traditional techniques with creative, modern twists, along with the attentive service. For a full experience, the omakase paired with sake or wine is highly recommended.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1553-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7725862,35.6904647,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Hinoya Curry Akihabara</name>
        <description>A good introduction to Japanese curry; it is a little spicy.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7703452,35.6991165,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>HIGUMA Doughnuts</name>
        <description>HIGUMA Doughnuts in Tokyo Dome City offers fluffy, delicious donuts that are highly praised for their light texture and gentle sweetness. </description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1607-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7543806,35.7050848,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Kantan-na Yume</name>
        <description>Kantan-na Yume feels like a hidden gem tucked away in a quiet part of the city. When I visited, the peaceful atmosphere struck me right away—a nice change from Shibuya just minutes away. The wagashi I tried were exquisite, beautifully made, and paired perfectly with a cup of matcha.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1607-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.6922418,35.6654512,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Homemade Ramen 麦苗</name>
        <description><![CDATA[f you're looking for an outstanding ramen experience, I highly recommend Homemade Ramen 麦苗. I visited recently, and despite not typically favoring shoyu broths, their version completely won me over with its depth and rich umami flavor. The noodles and toppings were exceptional—the steak in the deluxe toppings was some of the best I've had. Do note that you'll need to arrive early, around 9 or 10 AM, to put your name on the list due to its popularity. But trust me, the wait is worth it.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1640-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7328034,35.5907765,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>KOFFEE MAMEYA</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1534-FFEA00-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.710888,35.668368,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Gonpachi</name>
        <description><![CDATA['KILL BILL: VOLUME I' (2003) - The inspiration for the 'House of Blue Leaves']]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.723509,35.66012,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Ninja Tokyo</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFD600-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7671082,35.6849169,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Gyomu Super Asso-ekimae</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.418271,33.697278,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Yumune Chaya</name>
        <description>Boil some eggs and sweet potatoes in the public hot spring – best bought at Yumune Chaya, the teahouse adjacent to the Yumune Kusushi Tōkō-ji Temple (squeezed between the river and the public onsen) – they make great provisions for your hike.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.757742,33.828778,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Yamazaki Y Shop Shimoji Hongu</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.78886,33.821335,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Minamikata Shoten</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.842193,33.757788,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>A Coop Supermarket</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.609218,33.810334,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tororoya</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.609718,33.81022,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sansaro Cafe</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Sansaro Café & Guesthouse is a quiet and friendly stop along the Kumano route. The rooms are simple and clean, and the setting above the river is peaceful. There is no bath in the house, but the free ticket to Satsuki Onsen works well and is an easy walk. Supper and breakfast are cooked with care and fit the homestay feel. The owner is warm, helpful, and creates a comfortable atmosphere without trying too hard.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.874144,33.79765,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Onigiri Gorichan</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.5021961,34.6758595,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Masahiko Ozumi Paris</name>
        <description>Masahiko Ozumi Paris really lives up to its reputation for cakes that look like works of art. Each one is intricate, creative, and surprisingly delicious — not just pretty for photos. The flavors are refined, balanced, and not overly sweet, which I really appreciated.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1607-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.5123643,34.6862905,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sushi Shizuku Nishi-Temma</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1835-FFEA00-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.5030062,34.6960355,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Yakiniku Manno honten</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1553-FFEA00-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.5254846,34.6508673,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Yoshino Sushi</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1835-FFEA00-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.5018514,34.6871329,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Chibo Okonomiyaki</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.4926937,34.6986819,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Takoyaki Wanaka Dotonbori</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.5027298,34.6687742,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Rikuro’s Namba Main Branch</name>
        <description>Rikuro’s cheesecake is absolutely worth trying once — soft, jiggly, and more eggy and airy than the dense Western style. The raisins on the bottom aren’t for everyone, but they add a touch of sweetness that grew on me. The real drawback is the line: it moves slowly, and since they only sell whole cakes, it’s a commitment (especially for solo travelers).</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1607-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.501566,34.666127,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Ramen Yashichi</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1640-FFEA00-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.4997284,34.7112957,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Kappa Sushi</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1835-FFEA00-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.7711607,35.005657,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Honke Owariya Main Branch</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Visiting Japan’s “oldest soba restaurant” is more about the experience than just the food. The building and atmosphere are beautiful — traditional, calm, and steeped in history — and that’s something you can’t get just anywhere. Service was polite and efficient, even with the crowds.<br><br>As for the soba: it was good, but not mind-blowing.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1640-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.7601488,35.0127635,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>TENYU kyoto 天佑（てんゆう）京都</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.7753816,35.0007316,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>JOJOPUB</name>
        <description>A hidden gem by Kyoto Station! By day, the spice curry lunch is rich, balanced, and full of variety — the three-curry plate is a must. At night, it turns into a cozy bar with great drinks, friendly staff, and a relaxed vibe. Perfect for either a hearty meal or a few quiet cocktails.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.7491162,34.9843864,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Shin Shin</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1640-FFEA00-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            130.3968446,33.5927497,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tsuta</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1640-FFEA00-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.6824758,35.6703963,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>168 Gelato Caffe</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1607-FFEA00-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            134.993946,34.574921,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>naa yuu Cafe</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            130.520022,30.2350224,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Milky Way</name>
        <description>Stopped in after walking around the lake and the place really does feel like someone’s grandmother opened her living room to the public. Two older ladies run everything themselves and they are genuinely kind. The food is simple, homemade, and better than it looks. I would get the curry. It is the kind of mild, comforting Japanese curry that tastes like it has been made the same way for decades. Portions are modest but clean and satisfying. Cash only and no toilet inside, but it is a quiet, warm stop if you want a break from the tourist crowds.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1534-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            138.7543862,35.5107593,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Fuso Ann</name>
        <description><![CDATA[t’s tiny (just around 10 seats), run by one incredibly kind woman who cooks, serves, and welcomes you all at once. The menu is very limited — basically soba noodles and a handful of traditional desserts — but everything is fresh, simple, and prepared with real care.<br><br>The soba is comforting, light, and perfect after a walk by the lake. The desserts, especially the matcha mochi and anmitsu.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            138.7520947,35.5105071,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Gyoza no Umechan</name>
        <description>This little family-run spot in Nikko is the kind of restaurant you hope to stumble across while traveling. The menu is small and straightforward — ramen, curry, rice bowls, and of course their famous gyoza — but everything feels homemade and cooked with care. The gyoza are huge, juicy, and easily the standout dish, while the ramen has that warm, comforting “home kitchen” flavor you don’t get from chain shops.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1640-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.6167413,36.7479314,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Isshiki SAKANA HIROBA</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Isshiki Sakana Hiroba is the kind of market that shows you exactly what the local coast pulls in without dressing it up. Stalls are lined with fresh fish, dried seafood, and snacks, and the vendors are quick with samples even if communication stays simple. It is busy, loud, and direct in a way that feels honest rather than chaotic.<br><br>The real highlight is eating upstairs or at the small counters. Unagi, kaisendon, grilled seafood, and fried items are all straightforward and fresh.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1573-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            137.0141155,34.7944233,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Kanetaya</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Kanetaya is a long-running seafood restaurant with two different dining spaces on the same property. One side functions as a more formal Japanese dining room. The other operates like a casual restaurant with donburi, set meals, and hitsumabushi style dishes. Both sides use fish sourced from Choshi, and freshness is the dominant reason people go.<br><br>The menu tilts heavily toward raw fish, sardine dishes, eel, and mixed seafood bowls, plus seasonal items like oysters and bamboo shoots.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1573-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            140.2360335,35.842056,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Cafe Bocu</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Cafe Bocu sits just off the Kumano Kodo, tucked far enough into the trees that you start wondering if you missed the turn. It feels like someone’s countryside home that happens to serve lunch to the few people who know it exists. The room is quiet, the air smells like baking bread, and the view is nothing but green.<br><br>Food here is simple and careful. Light vegetable-forward plates, fresh bread coming out of the oven, the kind of lunch that feels clean and handmade rather than heavy.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.6098169,33.8174208,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>iro</name>
        <description>Really nice café along the river near Waseda with a quiet, relaxed vibe. The tea menu is excellent and the toast is solid, especially for a simple breakfast or afternoon break. It can fill up, and seating is compact, but the atmosphere makes it worth the effort. Service is consistently kind.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FFEA00-labelson</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7182778,35.7125957,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
    </Folder>
    <Folder>
      <name>Special Restaurants (reservations or wait)</name>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Karashibi Miso Ramen Kikanbō Kanda Honten</name>
        <description><![CDATA[—(Prepare to wait) popular for its intensely spicy Karashibi Miso Ramen. The interior, characterized by its black paint, taiko music, and festival-style devil masks, adds to the immersive dining experience. You can customize their spice level on a scale of five for both "kara" (chili heat) and "Shibi" (numbing spice from sansho pepper mix), catering to varying spice tolerances.<br>1.	Feel free to challenge my “Oni” title; we can sit side by side if you live that spicy life. 😉]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1640-FF5252</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7724063,35.6936985,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Udatsu Sushi</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Udatsu delivers the full package without a single wasted gesture. The service is precise, quiet, and attentive in the way the best Tokyo counters are, and the presentation is meticulous without ever feeling staged. Every piece was balanced, clean, and confidently prepared. The pairings, both sake and the small seasonal touches, were thoughtful rather than showy, and the pacing of the course made the meal feel effortless.<br><br>I have eaten a lot of sushi across Japan, and this is easily among the best I have had. Nothing here feels gimmicked or inflated. It is the craft itself, executed at an exceptionally high level. It is hard to imagine anyone walking away disappointed with this standard of work.<br><br>Reservation required.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1835-FF5252</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.6952875,35.641992,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tenryuji Temple Shigetsu</name>
        <description><![CDATA[The vegan set lunch at Tenryū-ji was beautifully balanced, seasonal, and surprisingly filling without ever feeling heavy. Every dish had subtle, layered flavors that showed real care in preparation.<br><br>The setting makes it unforgettable: sitting in view of the temple gardens and koi pond, the atmosphere is serene and quiet, adding to the sense that you’re eating something more meaningful than just food. Service was kind and efficient, though very little English is spoken, so patience and openness go a long way.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1804-FF5252</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.6735833,35.0152053,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>LATTE ART MANIA TOKYO</name>
        <description><![CDATA[If you're looking for a cool, creative coffee spot, Latte Art Mania is definitely worth a visit. I appreciated the attention to detail in their intricate latte designs. The space is small, but the energy is inviting, and the charcoal latte offers something unique—both in flavor and presentation.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1534-FF5252</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7164832,35.6711984,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Yakumo Saryō</name>
        <description><![CDATA[This serene and minimalist setting for a traditional Japanese breakfast was designed by the renowned architect Shinichiro Ogata. It provides a genuine cultural experience through its dining, highlighting Japanese cuisine as an art form involving various cultural elements, from the dishware to the chef’s etiquette. Yakumo Saryo offers a traditional Japanese breakfast called "Asacha," focusing on tea, priced at ¥4,000 (around $37). ]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FF5252</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.6675385,35.6189739,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>tonkatsu.jp Omotesando</name>
        <description><![CDATA[This place is doing something very deliberate with breed, fat, and fry, and the results make sense on the plate. The cut I chose was cooked to a precise medium, juicy without feeling heavy, and the crust was clean, crisp, and even.<br><br>Get a reservation.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FF5252</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.709991,35.6644248,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sanrokuen</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1764-FF5252-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            138.7677082,35.4934587,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Saijoen</name>
        <description>Saijoen feels like a quiet thesis statement on what Nishio matcha can be. The walk in through the garden sets the tone, and once you sit down the room is calm, unhurried, and focused on the tea and sweets rather than décor tricks. The matcha itself is rich and clear, with a bitterness that reads as depth rather than harshness. Soft serve, parfaits, and anmitsu all carry that same through line, so nothing tastes like a generic “green tea” dessert.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1607-FF5252</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            137.0400259,34.8776634,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Yakiman</name>
        <description>Local specialty: The eels come straight from Isshiki farms — thick, fresh, and fatty. Most diners recommend shirayaki (plain grilled) with condiments like lemon or umeboshi over the usual kabayaki sauce.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1573-FF5252</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            137.0295308,34.8045543,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Omen Ginkaku-ji</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Omen near Ginkaku-ji is a Kyoto classic — the kind of place where the atmosphere, service, and tradition make the visit as memorable as the food. The udon noodles are fresh, silky, and pair beautifully with the light, flavorful broth and seasonal vegetables. Tempura here is crisp and not oily, and the mackerel sushi is a standout side dish.<br><br>That said, it’s very popular, so be prepared to wait, especially around lunchtime.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1640-FF5252</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.7950089,35.0262562,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Soma Cafe</name>
        <description>A legitimately good stop if you want dessert or a proper coffee break while moving through Kiso-Fukushima. The place is a renovated old house, shoes off at the door, with a small gallery on the second floor selling local woodwork and ceramics. The staff actually speaks enough English to make ordering simple.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1577-FF5252</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            137.6988318,35.8501592,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
    </Folder>
    <Folder>
      <name>Exceptional Experiences</name>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Senbonto Shrine</name>
        <description>Nezu Shrine is where Tokyo finally shuts up for a minute. Turtles in the pond, koi cruising under that red bridge, a little tunnel of torii, and then the hillside that just erupts in azaleas late April–early May if you pay to go into the garden. Most of the year it’s just a calm neighborhood shrine between Ueno and Todai, with locals actually praying, a few food stalls on busy days, and almost none of the circus you get at Sensō-ji or Meiji.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7605557,35.7196347,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>DAWN | Avatar Robot Cafe ver.β</name>
        <description><![CDATA[DAWN isn’t a robot show. It’s a workaround for a society that still shuts people out. The pilots aren’t mascots. They’re people who want to work, earn money, and talk to customers but can’t physically show up in a building. The robots are the access ramp.<br><br>So when you sit there and a small white OriHime rolls up to your table, the point isn’t “look at the cute robot.” It’s the fact that someone at home, maybe in bed, maybe dealing with a condition that makes normal employment impossible, is still in the room with you. You’re not watching tech replace a worker. You’re watching tech give someone their job back.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7760205,35.6893781,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>teamLab Borderless: MORI Building DIGITAL ART MUSEUM</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E-nodesc</styleUrl>
