      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Shinjuku Golden-Gai</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7047611,35.6941118,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Godzilla Head</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7019117,35.6950521,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Edo-Tokyo Open Air Architectural Museum</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.5125434,35.7155722,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>The National Museum of Emerging Science and Innovation (Miraikan)</name>
        <description>If you care even a little about where science and society are heading, Miraikan is worth the train out to Odaiba. It’s less a “wow, robots everywhere” theme park and more a layered conversation about climate, aging, AI, and what kind of future we’re actually building. The giant Earth sphere floating in the atrium is as striking in person as the photos suggest, but the real value is in the interactive exhibits that make you think about data, bodies, and technology rather than just pressing buttons.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7763995,35.6193359,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Mori Art Museum</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7292785,35.6604621,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Toshima City Tokiwaso Manga Museum</name>
        <description>A small but pretty special stop if you care about manga history. The building is a full-scale reconstruction of the shabby postwar apartment where Tezuka Osamu, Fujiko Fujio, Ishinomori Shotaro and other Showa-era mangaka lived and worked in 4.5-tatami rooms, rushing pages to editors. Inside you walk through recreated rooms, shared kitchen and corridors, with exhibits on the neighborhood and the grind of weekly deadlines.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.6859984,35.7242899,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>TOKYO BUNBUN(東京文聞)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[I brought my film students to Tokyo BunBun as part of our Tokyo in Film program, and it turned into one of the best experiences we have had in Tokyo. Abe-san, Tomomi-san, and the whole BunBun team are generous, skilled, and completely invested in what they do. Their samurai choreography is precise and full of life, and they take the time to teach the real mechanics of movement, timing, and intent.<br><br>We filmed a full Musashi homage with them, inspired by Vagabond, and they welcomed us into their home so we could shoot the sequence properly. I was the primary videographer, editor, sound mixer, and narrator for the project, and the actors worked with us the way professionals work with professionals. The link shows the final result:<br>https://www.instagram.com/p/DQfI8VQj_ic/<br><br>If you care about samurai cinema, stage combat, Japanese performance traditions, or you simply want something real and memorable in Tokyo, this is the place. Tokyo BunBun is the team I trust.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.781992,35.7257206,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Fuji-Q Highland</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            138.7805511,35.4869467,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>LEGOLAND Discovery Center Osaka</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.4302448,34.656179,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Universal Studios Japan</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.4323185,34.6656768,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>teamLab Botanical Garden Osaka</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.5211348,34.6104441,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Naruto x Boruto park Nijigen no mori</name>
        <description>As a long-time Naruto fan, this felt like walking straight into the Leaf Village. The core of the park is the mission system: stamp rallies, hand-seal challenges, and the big wooden maze that has you ducking, squatting, and hunting for clues like a genin on a real assignment. It’s more physical than it looks, so be ready for stairs, heat, and very little shade in summer. The theming is loving rather than flashy, with plenty of statues, photo spots, and small details that reward you for slowing down instead of rushing through. Ichiraku-style ramen at the top of the hill is surprisingly solid theme-park food and hits especially well after the missions. Access can be confusing the first time—using the official shuttle bus from the ferry or highway bus stop is much easier than trying to improvise with taxis—so it works best as part of a full day on Awaji Island (often paired with the Godzilla attraction).</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.0046027,34.575579,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Godzilla Museum</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.0002411,34.5736731,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Godzilla Interception Operation Awaji</name>
        <description>A very specific kind of heaven for Godzilla fans. The setup is lean but focused: short intro film, the zipline run at the full-size Shin Godzilla, a shooting mission, and then a museum that walks you through the history and designs. The zipline into the mouth is ridiculous in the best way and the models in the museum feel like a love letter to the series rather than a cheap tie-in. It does take some effort to reach the park and the ticket price is not small, so it makes the most sense as part of a full day on Awaji Island with the other themed areas.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.0002155,34.5736268,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Awajishima Anime Park</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Nijigen no Mori is legitimately fun and the setting is gorgeous, but you need to understand the structure before you arrive. The park is spacious and pleasant to walk through, and then each major attraction is ticketed separately. That can get expensive fast, especially if you are trying to sample everything.<br><br>The right approach is to treat it like a menu. Choose one “big” experience you are excited about (Godzilla zipline is a common highlight), then add one smaller one and leave the rest for another visit. If you do it that way, the value makes sense and you are not stressed about rushing across the park all day.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.000751,34.575173,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Katsuoji</name>
        <description>One of the best day-trips from Osaka. Quiet mountain setting, tons of daruma everywhere, and the stamp route actually makes the visit fun instead of gimmicky. Go early to avoid lines. If you’re already doing Minoh Falls, adding this temple makes the whole day much better.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.491087,34.8657752,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Hello Kitty Smile</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Very cute Sanrio themed stop on Awaji, with lots of photo spots and a few light interactive elements. The staff are friendly and the view is excellent, and the cafe is a solid bonus.<br><br>The main caveat is value. It is not a huge facility, so if you are expecting a full theme park scale experience, you will be done quickly. Treat it as a themed photo and cafe stop, not a half day attraction, and it makes a lot more sense.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            134.9525045,34.5671571,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Shogun Stables</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.6137308,36.7580611,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Kubota Itchiku Art Museum</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            138.7598324,35.527324,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>NARUTO×BORUTO FUJI HIDDEN VILLAGE OF KONOHA</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            138.7819971,35.4877001,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Shinobi No Sato Ninja Village</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            138.8180809,35.4567699,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Nishio</name>
        <description><![CDATA["Little Kyoto"]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            137.0618775,34.8619053,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Matcha Museum Saijoen WAKUWAKU</name>
        <description>If you are already in Nishio for Saijoen, the Matcha Museum next door is the part that makes the visit feel complete. The guided course is about 75 minutes and reservation only, so it is not a casual walk in, but once you are inside it is quiet, focused, and surprisingly hands on.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            137.0397789,34.877466,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Saku Island</name>
        <description>22 permanent modern art installations scattered across a quiet, rustic island. Super good for wandering with a camera.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            137.0400589,34.7289853,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Edo Wonderland Nikko Edomura</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Edo Wonderland Nikko Edomura is one of the strongest locations in the region if your goal is recreating Edo period scenes. The streetscapes read convincingly on camera, and there are multiple zones that give you usable depth, texture, and period detail without constant modern contamination if you frame carefully.<br><br>This is also a working theme park, so the experience depends on crowd level. Go early, and weekdays are easier for clean shots. The staff commitment to character helps the atmosphere, and the live performances add real energy even if you do not speak Japanese.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.6973346,36.7908146,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Boso Village</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Excellent set like open air museum for Edo period style scene recreation. The buildings and streets give you a lot to work with, especially if you shoot tight and pay attention to angles. Some areas are more active than others depending on the day, so check what is open if you are coming for specific workshops or demonstrations.<br><br>Worth the trip from Narita if you want period locations and good photo opportunities. Bring comfortable shoes, and avoid peak sun if you plan to shoot.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            140.2730663,35.8228912,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Kuchu Teien Observatory</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Kuchu Teien is worth it for one reason: Osaka looks best from above, and this building gives you the cleanest 360 view without turning it into a theme park. The escalators suspended between the two towers are the thing people remember, but the real payoff is the roof. It’s open air, windy, and you actually feel how high you are.<br><br>Go at sunset, stay until the city switches on, and ignore the heart-locks and tourist fluff. It’s one of the few places where Osaka’s scale, rivers, highways, and neon all make sense in a single frame.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.490172,34.7052993,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Kashihara</name>
        <description>The birth place of Street Fighter. See life sized statues, paintings, grate covers, etc., all in homage to the game that was born here.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            135.7925947,34.50945,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>The Waseda International House of Literature (The Haruki Murakami Library)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[The Haruki Murakami Library is one of my favorite places in Tokyo. I have visited many times and it never feels like a novelty or a tourist stop. It is a quiet, carefully designed space that understands mood, pacing, and restraint.<br><br>Murakami is one of my favorite authors, and the library reflects his work without turning it into branding. The music room playing jazz from the novels is especially well done and actually shapes how you experience the space rather than feeling decorative.<br><br>The café is good and makes it easy to stay for a long time reading or thinking. This is a place built for reflection, not crowds. Highly recommended for readers and anyone interested in literature.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1882-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7199006,35.7097263,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
    </Folder>
    <Folder>
      <name>Accommodation</name>
      <Placemark>
        <name>BnA_WALL - Art Hotel in Tokyo</name>
        <description>Staying here feels less like a standard hotel and more like sleeping inside a piece of installation art. Each room is a completely different concept, but underneath the design the basics are solid: comfortable bed, very clean space, strong in-room Wi-Fi, and a surprisingly good shower and bathroom for a compact Tokyo room. The neighborhood is quiet but central enough that you can reach most parts of the city easily by subway, and coming back here at night feels like stepping off the grid a bit.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1602-0288D1</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7778077,35.688766,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
