      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Minshuku Murou</name>
        <description>The house is a beautifully renovated old kominka on a hill, with big windows looking out over tiled roofs, rice fields, and Mt. Muro—on a misty morning it really does feel like the “dragon” landscape locals talk about. Meals are vegetable-forward, seasonal, and clearly cooked with care; it’s the kind of food that makes sense because you’re in the countryside, not in spite of it. The baths are simple but relaxing, the rooms are spotless and calm, and the owners are kind in a very grounded, non-hotel way—happy to pick you up, talk about the area, and help you plan a visit to Muro-ji or the dragon god spots.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1602-0288D1</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            136.035002,34.536639,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name><![CDATA[Kaido-Roman On'yado Tsutaya]]></name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1602-0288D1-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            137.697966,35.8503514,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Daikichi</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Daikichi is exactly what you want at the end of a Nakasendo walking day. It sits just off the main street in Tsumago, quiet once the day trippers leave, and feels like staying in a family home that has been refined over decades. The rooms are simple and clean, with thick futons and views that remind you you are in the mountains, not on a tourist circuit. The shared hinoki bath is small but genuinely relaxing after the trail.<br><br>The real reason to stay here is the hospitality and the food. The hosts are attentive without fuss, and the meals are careful, local, and just adventurous enough to remind you you are in the countryside rather than in a hotel dining room.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1602-0288D1</styleUrl>
        <Point>
