      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Meiji Jingu</name>
        <description><![CDATA[description: Huge Shinto shrine complex in the middle of the city, wrapped in a forest that actually feels separate from Tokyo once you pass the giant torii. The long gravel approach is the point: quiet, tree-lined, and a reset after Shibuya and Harajuku, even though it can still be crowded. The main shrine is simple and spacious rather than ornate, with wedding processions and children in formal dress common on weekends. Entrance to the grounds is free; the Inner Garden and treasure museum charge small separate fees and are worth it if you want more time in nature, a lily pond, and a bit of sword and Meiji history.<br>説明: <br>nom: ]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1804-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="description">
            <value>Huge Shinto shrine complex in the middle of the city, wrapped in a forest that actually feels separate from Tokyo once you pass the giant torii. The long gravel approach is the point: quiet, tree-lined, and a reset after Shibuya and Harajuku, even though it can still be crowded. The main shrine is simple and spacious rather than ornate, with wedding processions and children in formal dress common on weekends. Entrance to the grounds is free; the Inner Garden and treasure museum charge small separate fees and are worth it if you want more time in nature, a lily pond, and a bit of sword and Meiji history.</value>
          </Data>
          <Data name="説明">
            <value/>
          </Data>
          <Data name="nom">
            <value/>
          </Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.6993259,35.6763976,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Fukagawa Fudou-dō (Naritasan Tokyo branch temple)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[description: If you only have time for one temple experience in Tokyo, make it this one. The main hall is a modern concrete space, but the goma fire ceremony is intense and unforgettable: monks chanting, taiko drums shaking the floor, conch shells, and columns of flame rising in front of Fudō Myōō. Services run several times a day, and it is worth sitting through the full ritual. Take off your shoes, keep your phone put away, and just let the sound and smoke wash over you.<br>説明: <br>nom: ]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1804-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="description">
            <value>If you only have time for one temple experience in Tokyo, make it this one. The main hall is a modern concrete space, but the goma fire ceremony is intense and unforgettable: monks chanting, taiko drums shaking the floor, conch shells, and columns of flame rising in front of Fudō Myōō. Services run several times a day, and it is worth sitting through the full ritual. Take off your shoes, keep your phone put away, and just let the sound and smoke wash over you.</value>
          </Data>
          <Data name="説明">
            <value/>
          </Data>
          <Data name="nom">
            <value/>
          </Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7983222,35.6728891,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tomioka Hachiman Shrine</name>
        <description><![CDATA[description: A big, atmospheric neighborhood shrine with a lot more history than you expect when you step through the main torii. The grounds are spacious and calm most days, but you can feel how important this place is to the area in the sumo monuments, the massive mikoshi displays, and the festival notices posted around the precincts.<br>説明: <br>nom: ]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1804-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="description">
            <value>A big, atmospheric neighborhood shrine with a lot more history than you expect when you step through the main torii. The grounds are spacious and calm most days, but you can feel how important this place is to the area in the sumo monuments, the massive mikoshi displays, and the festival notices posted around the precincts.</value>
          </Data>
          <Data name="説明">
            <value/>
          </Data>
          <Data name="nom">
            <value/>
          </Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
