      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>GINZA TSUTAYA BOOKS</name>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1684-BDBDBD-nodesc</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.764267,35.669416,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Gashapon Ikebukuro Main Store</name>
        <description>The largest small capsule store in the world. Over 3000 machines.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1684-BDBDBD</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.719795,35.728468,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Nishio City Iwase Archives</name>
        <description>Nishio City Iwase Archives is one of those places you only understand once you walk through the door. It looks small from the outside, but inside is a concentrated, carefully kept collection that feels impossible to find anywhere else. Edo-period books, early prints, publishing materials, and facsimiles you can actually handle are all presented without fuss or theatrical staging. It is quiet, serious, and incredibly well maintained.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1526-BDBDBD</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            137.0539847,34.8742708,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Mikawakogei Glass Museum</name>
        <description>Mikawakogei Glass Museum is small, tucked into a quiet residential area, and more like stepping into an artist’s house than a formal museum. Once you are inside, it works. The stained glass pieces and glass objects have a handmade, personal feel, and the big draw is the kaleidoscope spaces.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1526-BDBDBD</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            137.0242838,34.8532438,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name><![CDATA[Jazz & Soul Bar - The Deep]]></name>
        <description>Tiny room, grand piano, and you are close enough to hear the singers breathe between phrases. This feels less like a club and more like being invited into someone’s living room to sit with the band. I came for one set and stayed for the second, sipping whiskey and watching the pianist and vocalist trade energy at arm’s length. Staff and regulars were kind even with my clumsy Japanese, and the music charge felt more than fair for that level of playing.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1637-BDBDBD</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7607358,35.6712082,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Mandarake Complex</name>
        <description>Mandarake Complex is Akihabara in concentrated form: eight packed floors of second-hand manga, figures, gunpla, trading cards, retro games, doujinshi, and every flavor of niche collectible. Stock turns over constantly, so it is the kind of place you can revisit and still stumble onto rare or out-of-print items, from Macross mecha and Digimon merch to vintage model kits and “spicy” adult floors tucked higher up.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1526-BDBDBD</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7705031,35.7003733,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>AKIHABARA KOTOBUKIYA</name>
        <description>Kotobukiya is the polished, curated counterpoint to Akihabara’s chaos. Instead of dusty bins and mystery shelves, you get a bright, well laid out multi-floor shop where everything is grouped by series and medium: JRPG merch, Monster Hunter, Final Fantasy, Zelda, Ghibli, Persona, Tales, VTuber floors devoted to Hololive and Nijisanji, plus a serious spread of figures and plastic model kits. It is all new stock rather than second hand, so you are paying for condition and presentation rather than gambling on thrift.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1684-BDBDBD</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7704606,35.6992291,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Akihabara Radio Kaikan</name>
        <description>Ten floors of pure Akiba chaos. Figures, cards, model kits, gacha, and every kind of hobby shop stacked on top of each other. Crowded, cramped, and completely part of the fun. Prices jump around depending on the floor, so look before you buy, but if you want to understand why people come to Akihabara in the first place, this building explains it in one hit.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1684-BDBDBD</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7719571,35.6979149,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>TAMASHII NATIONS STORE TOKYO</name>
        <description>Small, clean, and set up like a showroom more than a shop. The displays are the real draw here. Gundam, Kamen Rider, Dragon Ball, Saint Seiya, Metal Build, all staged in museum-level setups. Stock is limited and some of the big releases sell out fast, so temper your expectations. Still, if you are already in Akihabara, this is one of the best places to see the newest Bandai figures up close before you decide what to hunt for elsewhere.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1684-BDBDBD</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7729878,35.6991106,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Blue Note Tokyo</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Blue Note Tokyo is one of the few places that really lives up to its reputation. The room is intimate, the sound is precise without being harsh, and every seat feels designed around the stage rather than the other way around. It is closer to a small theatre than a bar, which suits the level of musicians they bring through here.<br><br>Service is efficient and professional, from the foyer check-in to the last drink. The staff handle a full house without breaking the atmosphere, and the whole operation feels rehearsed in a good way. The food is fine but not the reason to come.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1637-BDBDBD</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.7160849,35.6611386,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Mandarake Shibuya</name>
        <description>Mandarake Shibuya feels less like a shop and more like an underground archive. You descend into a basement that looks and smells like a dungeon, then realize it just keeps going: aisles packed with used manga, vintage toys, figures, CDs, doujinshi, and all kinds of pop-culture relics. The layout is tight and can get warm and crowded, so it is not a casual browse; you have to be willing to take your time and dig. Prices are not always “bargain bin,” but the strength of this place is depth and weirdness rather than discounts.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1684-BDBDBD</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.6977247,35.661535,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Toy Sapiens</name>
        <description>Flagship Hot Toys store that feels closer to a small museum than a shop. Floor is packed with Marvel and Star Wars sixth-scale figures, life-size statues, and beautifully lit display cases that are fun even if you never buy a thing. The downside is that most of the “wow” pieces are sold out, and DC or comics-based figures are thin compared to the movie lines.</description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1684-BDBDBD</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            139.702665,35.6655919,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
    </Folder>
  </Document>
